Date: Tue, 04 Jan 1994 14:19:51 -0230
From: Bill Garland <bill@kean.ucs.mun.ca>
Subject: Soaking the hapless taxpayers

Hi again.

My second question about a startup involves a question of extropian
morality. Having become an anarchist over these past few months, I
would just as soon never deal with any governments again. This is
not possible yet, of course.

Meanwhile, in my attempt to rise out of a condition that seemed to
be everlasting, namely chronic poverty, I have started several
businesses. I was not able to get anything going well enough to
stave off personal bankruptcy, but my businesses survived since
they had few assets and by having invested $50 I had incorporated
my business and thus protected it from myself.

Anyway, I have written a traditional-style Business Plan, for my
computer services company, and wrote down some of my ideas for
getting this business going, for a venture-capitalist audience.
I had a friend who knows many vc's and people in high places
look over this plan, and with a few minor edits, not a major
rewrite, he figures it would be fairly easy for me to walk into
some government offices and walk out with about $250,000 of 
money extorted from hapless taxpayers (my new favourite phrase).
I could use this money as salary for a year, salary for a 
secretary/assistant/bookkeeper/slave, computer and office
equipment, and so on. Then, after a year of R&D, I could begin
marketing products and services and go to real venture capitalists
for real money to expand into the global market and get rich.

There are several problems with this, of course. Namely, as
mentioned in the startup virtsem, this is a backward approach.
Marketing is everything, and doing it this wimpy way will 
guarantee failure, not success, and will have wasted the $250K.
No real vc's would provide investment capital for a year of
R&D, as Tim May has pointed out. The idea of using the year to
polish up the software products, install the gear, find customers,
etc, is all a waste. I need marketable products right from the
start, and I don't want to hire a gaggle of lackeys just to
say I created some jobs. Furthermore, I don't like dealing with
politicians and bureaucrats and wouldn't want to owe anyone of
them anything.

But if there is such free money available, shouldn't I take it?

My competitors would certainly take it. If they can use this
money to go after my market, they will out-compete me and win
the Customers' dollars. It thus becomes a survival issue.
Even if I do have a properly designed marketing strategy with
some real products and services to sell, should I not take a
grab at this free money as a way to help enrich myself?

It is unlikely that I can develp my products to industrial strength
without taking some leave of absence from my day job, so this
would provide an ideal opportunity to go for it.

The only millionaire I know personally is a man who quit his job,
sold his house, cashed in his pension, and was on the street with
his wife and $5000 cash. With four youngsters to feed and somewhat
less gumption than this friend, I cannot see myself doing that.
Besides, as a bankrupt, if they ever found out I cashed in my
pension, they'd seize it. I have to wait another three months or
so before I get discharged from bankruptcy, and I am not too
keen on cashing in this pension money anyway.

If the government knew that what I wanted to do with the money
was set up a digital bank and an a/c marketplace, with offshore
tax havens and cryptographically secure transactions, they'd 
never give me the this free money anyway, but all this is part
of the hidden agenda...he computer services and EDI/POS/EFT
and UDEM marketing are all real stuff.

So the question is, should I take the money and run?

     god_ii

