Date: Tue, 4 Jan 1994 11:41:01 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Digital banking

(I apologize to Bill for any harshness of tone here or in my last
post. I just can't hold back when I see what I think are
misperceptions of reality. I hope Bill takes my comments for what they
are, honest comments.)

I just commented on Bill Garland's ideas about getting a $250K grant
from the Canadian government, so I'll limit my responses here to the
issues of digital banks, cryptography, etc. I'm always happy to see
crypto come up here on the Extropians list....the Cyherpunks list has
taken much of the traffic away. That, and the departure of former
crypt-heads Hal Finney, Marc Ringuette, and others.

Bill writes:

> Too bad the start-up virtual seminar died out so quickly. 
> As I mentioned briefly in a previous message, I want to start using
> an new .sig, a draft of which is used below, for Extropian eyes only.

Actually, the Start-Up VirtSem ran a fairly full course, of a least 2
weeks. For virtual seminars, that's a long time. (And I just posted
another message several days back on it, but it got no response, so I
guess the thread is over for now.)


> I had targeted Jan 1/94 as a date to start doing some of the things 
> noted in my .sig. that I eventually want to do in a big way, such as 
> starting a private digital cash bank, providing an internet gateway 
> to my customers, doing EDI and other transaction processing, marketing 

This is a tall order! Setting up a digital bank has _never_ been done
before. I know of several groups that are _talking_ about doing it,
but none has so far done it. The cryptographic protocols are far from
complete, though they may be adequate for certain classes of banking.
That is, there's a range of possible banking services, some of which
require no crypto (except the normal kind), some of which require more
sophisticated protocols, and some of which apparently will require
as-yet-unrefined protocols.

If Bill or anyone else is to do this, it would help if they had access
to world-class talent in cryptology, preferably of the "digital money"
flavor. Currently most of that expertise is connected to David Chaum's
group in Amsterdam, though tendrils extend into Berkeley, M.I.T., and
a few other places.

The Cypherpunks and IMP-Interest groups have some of these folks---and
rumor has it that some of _them_ are pursuing the digital banking
ideas. We'll see what happens.

Since I don't want to sound like a dynamic pessimist, I'll leave it
for Bill and others to draw conclusions about the chance of someone in
Newfoundland to pull of the coup of deploying a robust digital bank.

> my UEDM software products,and so on. These are only dreams at the 
> moment, as I have very much research to do (Extropian homework). 
> I'll be lucky if I can get my bank startup off the ground this 
> millenium, let alone this year. However, if I go ahead and start
> planning this business out loud, maybe it'll get me off my ass...

Start by mastering all of Schneier's book on crypto (which I haven't
done, by the way....only so much time is available), and be reading
all of the past "Crypto" Proceedings. 

> I sent for a subscription to IMP-Interest, so I can see just how
> far I have to go in the research department. However, I have an
> idea that I'd like to run by you.
> 
> I believe that anybody can start and run a digital cash bank,
> assuming the security problems can be solved (and that users of
> public key cryptography are not all put up against the wall and
> shot). Not everyone will want to do this, however, so the ones
> who do will provide services to the ones who don't. To make
> things work properly, there will have to be anonymous remailers
> and Chaumian mixes available all over the place for anarchocapitalists
> to use. So if we develop a standard way to implement these devices,
> and freely distribute the software, or sell it for a small fee, to
> any extropian/cypherpunk/anarchocapitalist to use, there will be
> money to be made. Perry Metzger warned us of all the six-figure
> analysts employed on Wall Street who are doing this type of work
> for the conventional banks, so as not to take these matters too
> lightly, but I'm willing to speculate that we can do this ourselves,
> extropian geniuses that we all are...

These things won't just happen spontaneously, Extropian principles
aside. 

> Perhaps this is what imp-interest is all about. I don't know how
> much more mail I can handle. I had to drop cyperpunks because of
> the volume and more because of the detweiller noise, and I just
> checked out talk.politics.crypto, and the detweiller perversion
> has invaded that space, too, rendering it very noisy. But maybe
> all these details are addressed there (imp-interest, that is).

I'm sorry you dropped the Cyherpunks list instead of just hitting
"delete" for posts you don't want to read. That list, like this one,
is not noise-free, but you have now cut yourself off from people you
really need to be in contact with if you really expect to start a
digital bank.

> What I need to do is buy a machine on which to run my system.
> I was thinking of an alpha, running unix, although I _hate_
> unix. (I'd rather use OS/8 on a PDP-8.) The prices seem almost 
...rest elided....

I think you're getting way ahead of yourself. You don't buy
specialized hardware and _then_ figure out what to do with it.

My guess is that if you're a programming genius, a couple of years
spent studying the crypto literature, the past work on digital money,
and trying out some ideas, _might_ result in a breakthrough on your
part that _could_ lead to a successful startup. 

Is it hopeless? No, as there _are_ programming geniuses out there,
somewhere, and over the next half dozen years, some of them will solve
the remaining problems with digital money protocols. Perhaps they're
now coding away at Credit Suisse or for Marc Rich. Perhaps some clever
grad students of Chaum or Goldwasser will deploy a "toy" system on a
university network somewhere (as the precursors to Sun Microsystems
and Cisco Systems were deployed at Stanford). Then some folks,
including the geniuses, will "commercialize" the system.

The lone inventor is very unlikely to leap ahead of the cutting-edge
research now being done. Yes, it _could_ happen, but I wouldn't bet
money on it.

